\label{prop:mixed-timing}
Let $m=d^5$ and let $f^\star_d$ be the two-mode target \eqref{eq:two-mode-target} with $w_d=Bd^{-5}$ for a constant $B>0$. Then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mixed-timing-orders}
\overline{\mathcal L}_{\rm TE}\asymp d^{-3},\qquad
\inf_{t\ge0}\overline{\mathcal L}_{\rm ST}(t)\asymp d^{-5},
\end{equation}
and the upper bound is attained at $t=x/\sigma_4^2$ for every fixed $x>0$, with constants depending on $x$. Consequently
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mixed-timing-ratios}
\frac{\inf_t\overline{\mathcal L}_{\rm ST}(t)}{\overline{\mathcal L}_{\rm TE}}\asymp d^{-2}\longrightarrow0,
\qquad
\frac{\inf_t\overline{\mathcal L}_{\rm ST}(t)}{\overline{\mathcal L}_{\rm TE}^{\,2}}\asymp d\longrightarrow\infty :
\end{equation}
weak-to-strong generalization occurs, but no stopping time gives quadratic improvement. The best improvement exponent is $5/3$.

\label{lem:general-coefficients}
\leavevmode
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\operatorname{tr}\Psi=\sum_{k\ge0}N_k\sigma_k^2=\mathbb E\bigl[\sigma(\sqrt d\,\langle u,x\rangle)^2\bigr]\to\mathbb E[\sigma(z)^2]$ as $d\to\infty$. In particular $\operatorname{tr}\Psi\lesssim1$.
\item[(ii)] For each fixed $k$, $N_k\sigma_k^2\to c_k^2$ as $d\to\infty$. In particular $\sigma_k^2\asymp d^{-k}$ for each fixed $k\in\mathcal K_\sigma$.
\item[(iii)] If $\sigma$ is odd, then $\sigma_k=0$ for every $d$ and every even $k$. If $\sigma$ is even, then $\sigma_k=0$ for every $d$ and every odd $k$.
\end{enumerate}

\label{rem:general-activation-scope}
The hypothesis $k_0\in\mathcal K_\sigma$ cannot be dropped. If $\sigma$ has the parity opposite to that of $k_0$, then $\sigma_{k_0}=0$ for every $d$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:general-coefficients}(iii). The target is then orthogonal to every feature, so the teacher's best fit is zero; the student, which starts at zero and is trained on the teacher, stays at zero; and both errors equal one at all times. The hypothesis $c_{k_\star}\ne0$ for some $k_\star>\alpha$ fails only for a polynomial $\sigma$ of degree $p\le\alpha$. Then $\sigma_k=0$ for every $k>p$ and every $d$, so $\Psi$ has rank at most $\sum_{k\le p}N_k\asymp d^p$. For $\alpha>p$ this is smaller than $m$, the fixed point \eqref{eq:main-fixed-point} has no solution, and the teacher represents every target in the span of the features exactly.

The proof uses Lemma~\ref{lem:general-coefficients} only through $\operatorname{tr}\Psi\lesssim1$ and $N_k\sigma_k^2\asymp1$ at the two degrees $k_0$ and $k_\star$. It uses neither the exact values of the $\sigma_k$, nor monotonicity of $\sigma_k^2$ in $k$, nor any rate of decay of the $c_k$. We expect the stopping-time phases of Theorem~\ref{theo:phases} and the mixed-target examples of Appendix~\ref{app:mixed-examples} to carry over in the same way, with $\mathcal K$ replaced by $\mathcal K_\sigma$, since they use only the orders of $N_k$ and $\sigma_k^2$; we have not written this out.
